<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivan Bunin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a>–<a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ivan Alekseyevich Bunin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivan Bunin was a Russian writer, born in Voronezh in 1870 into an old noble family, who in 1933 became the first Russian writer awarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature. He carried on the classical traditions of Russian prose and poetry, and is best known for the short novels The Village (1910) and Dry Valley (1912), the autobiographical novel The Life of Arseniev and the stories of Dark Avenues (1946). Hostile to Bolshevism, he settled in 1920 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and Grasse, where his diary of 1918–1920, Cursed Days, began to appear in 1925; during the German occupation he sheltered fugitives, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, in his house in Grasse. He died in Paris in 1953.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-bunin.jpg" alt="Ivan Bunin" width="330" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Иван_Бунин_(1928).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
